# DORA level-2 technical standard — ICT risk management framework (Title II) and simplified ICT risk
# management framework (Title III). Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2024/1774 of 13 March 2024
# supplementing Regulation (EU) 2022/2554 with regard to regulatory technical standards specifying ICT risk
# management tools, methods, processes, and policies and the simplified ICT risk management framework.
# OJ L, 2024/1774, 25.6.2024. CELEX 32024R1774. ELI http://data.europa.eu/eli/reg_del/2024/1774/oj
# Entry into force: the twentieth day following publication (Art.42) — 15 July 2024 by that rule (computed,
# not printed). The ESA draft reference JC 2023 86 is from the commissioning brief; it is not printed in the act.
#
# Source: https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:32024R1774 (EUR-Lex English HTML,
#   retrieved 2026-10-13), sha256 18c36a91804c7901a89bfc04d9f397b734715484fb9832437fd06ac81659c124.
#   EU legal text, reusable under Commission Decision 2011/833/EU. Every row below was generated from that
#   HTML's article/paragraph/point structure (ELI subdivision ids art_N, NNN.MMM); text is carried as printed,
#   whitespace normalised.
#
# Rows: 308 = Title II 228 + Title III 80 (Title I and Title IV carry none, see Excluded).
#   Title II   Chapter I 143  Chapter II 12  Chapter III 19  Chapter IV 41  Chapter V 13
#   Title III  Chapter I  28  Chapter II 29  Chapter III 14  Chapter IV  9
#
# Id scheme: "Art.<article>(<paragraph>)<point>" exactly as the act numbers them, e.g. Art.7(2), Art.2(2)(c).
#   - A paragraph with lettered points is one row per point; its introductory words are carried once in
#     `leads` and printed with each point. Roman and arabic sub-points are folded into their point's text.
#   - Articles 3, 13, 19, 21, 22, 33, 34, 35 and 37 print no paragraph number; the act cites them as
#     "Article 3, first subparagraph, point (a)" (see Art.11(2)(f)(iii), Art.18(2)(a)), so that first
#     subparagraph is keyed (1): Art.3(1)(a), Art.21(1)(g).
#   - A later subparagraph that begins "For the purposes of point (x)" is folded into row (x) of the same
#     paragraph, marked "[Second subparagraph:]" etc. (Art.3's second list, "for the purposes of the first
#     paragraph, point (c)", is in Art.3(1)(c); Art.11(2)'s list for point (k) is in Art.11(2)(k)).
#   - Any other later subparagraph is its own row "Art.<a>(<p>), <ordinal> subparagraph", with its list
#     folded in. Lettering ambiguity: Art.16(2) and Art.17(2) each reuse (a)-(c) (central counterparties)
#     and (a)-(e) (central securities depositories) inside one paragraph; those lists are therefore not
#     separate rows but sit in Art.16(2)/Art.17(2), second subparagraph and third subparagraph.
#   - Art.29(2) second subparagraph ("The ICT security measures shall include all of the measures referred to
#     in Articles 30 to 38") is a cross-reference, folded into Art.29(2).
# Excluded (no row): Art.1 (Title I, proportionality — the frame both titles are read under, not a separately
#   attestable duty; the brief's "Title II ... Articles 1-27" is read as Articles 2-27, which is what Title II
#   holds), Art.16(6) and Art.28(3) (permissions: "may"), Art.42 (Title IV, entry into force — no entity duty).
# Printed as in the source (not corrected): "promp detection" (Art.3(1)(e)(iii)); "Regulation (EU) 2022/2254"
#   (Art.4(2)(b)(iii)); "2022/2554.;" (Art.27(2)(c)); "asses" (Art.36(2)); the trailing comma closing
#   Art.17(2)'s central-counterparty list; and Art.21's "For the purposes of point (e)(i)" sentence, which sets a
#   log-retention period although point (e)(i) is about access-right roles (folded into Art.21(1)(e) as printed).
#
# Input contract (input.dora.rts):
#   simplified_framework  true | false, MUST be declared. true -> Title III (financial entities under
#                         Article 16(1) of Regulation (EU) 2022/2554); false -> Title II. Absent or non-boolean
#                         is a violation (fail-closed) and Title II is evaluated.
#   central_counterparty, central_securities_depository, trading_venue, microenterprise
#                         true | false, MUST be declared when Title II is evaluated (each undeclared one is a
#                         violation). They open the rows the act addresses to those entities only (Art.16(2),
#                         17(2), 24(2)-(4), 25(3)-(4)) and close the two "other than microenterprises" points
#                         (Art.4(2)(c), Art.25(2)(c)). Not read for Title III.
#   obligations           {"Art.3(1)(a)": true, ...}. Missing or non-object -> FAIL-CLOSED violation. A
#                         non-true value on an evaluated row is a violation; an id that is not a row is a
#                         violation; an id of the title not being evaluated is accepted and ignored.
#
# Relationship to dora.main: dora.main's Pillar 1 checks cite Regulation (EU) 2022/2554 itself (Arts 5-13);
# none cited this RTS, so nothing was re-keyed. Every message here starts "DORA RTS 2024/1774", so the
# Level-1 pillar buckets ("DORA Art.9:" ...) never pick these up and no row is counted twice.
#
# OPA endpoint: POST /v1/data/dora/rts_ict_risk/compliance_report

# METADATA
# title: "DORA level-2 technical standard — ICT risk management framework and simplified framework (RTS (EU) 2024/1774)"
# custom:
#   class: compliance
#   framework: dora
#   source: eu
#   domains: [financial, eu]
#   coverage:
#     checks: 313
#     cited: 309
#     expected: 308
#     found: 313
#     scope: module
#     density: full
#     basis: declared-total
#     tested: true
#     entrypoint: compliance_report
#     guard: self
#     as_of: v2.4.2
package dora.rts_ict_risk

import rego.v1

default compliant := false

compliant if {
	count(violation) == 0
}

